The '''2016 UEFA Europa League final''' was a [[association football|football]] match between [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] of England and [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] of Spain on 18 May 2016 at [[St. Jakob-Park]] in [[Basel]], Switzerland. The showpiece event was the final match of the [[2015–16 UEFA Europa League]], the 45th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]].<ref name="2016 host">{{cite web |title=Basel awarded 2016 UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c510497d14c-3300ce93cbc8-1000--basel-awarded-2016-uefa-europa-league-final/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 September 2014 |access-date=13 March 2016 }}</ref> Liverpool were appearing in their fourth final, after their appearances in [[1973 UEFA Cup Final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup Final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Cup Final|2001]], all of which they won. Sevilla were appearing in their fifth final and third in succession. They had appeared in [[2006 UEFA Cup Final|2006]] and [[2007 UEFA Cup Final|2007]], as well as the previous two finals in [[2014 UEFA Europa League Final|2014]] and [[2015 UEFA Europa League Final|2015]], winning all four.

Liverpool entered the competition in the group stages, while Sevilla started in the round of 32 after finishing third in their Champions League group. Liverpool's ties in the knockout phase ranged from close affairs to comfortable victories. A last-minute goal against [[Borussia Dortmund]] of Germany secured a 5–4 aggregate victory in the quarter-finals, while they beat [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] of Spain 3–1 over two legs in the semi-final. Sevilla's matches were similar. They beat the Swiss team [[FC Basel]] 3–0 on aggregate in the Round of 16, but their quarter-final match with fellow Spanish team [[Athletic Bilbao]] went to a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], which they won 5–4 after the tie had finished 3–3 over two-legs.

Watched by a crowd of 34,429, Liverpool took the lead in the 35th minute when striker [[Daniel Sturridge]] scored. However, they conceded within the first minute of the second half when Sevilla striker [[Kevin Gameiro]] levelled the match. Sevilla took the lead in the 64th minute when captain [[Coke (footballer)|Coke]] scored. They extended their lead six minutes later when Coke scored his second of the match. Liverpool were unable to respond during the remainder of the match which meant Sevilla won the match 3–1 to win the competition for the fifth time, and third in succession.

Sevilla earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League]], [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], in the [[2016 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified for the group stage of the [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League]], as Real Madrid also qualified for the group stage through domestic performance, that meant the berth reserved for the Champions League title holders was not used and passed to the Europa League title holders.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid=2215121.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150228084925/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/newsid=2215121.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=February 28, 2015|title=How the Europa League winners will enter the Champions League|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=6 May 2016}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:St-Jakob-Park.jpg|thumb|The St. Jakob-Park in Basel was selected to host the final in September 2014.]]
The [[St. Jakob-Park]] was announced as the venue of the final at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Nyon]], Switzerland, on 18 September 2014.<ref name="2016 host"/> This was the first European club final hosted at the stadium, although the previous stadium of the same name, the [[St. Jakob Stadium]], which opened in 1954 for the [[1954 FIFA World Cup]] and closed in 1998, hosted four [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]] finals in [[1969 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1969]], [[1975 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1975]], [[1979 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1979]] and [[1984 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1984]].

The current St. Jakob-Park, nicknamed "Joggeli" by fans, was built in 1998 and opened in 2001, and is the home stadium of [[FC Basel|Basel]]. It hosted six matches at [[UEFA Euro 2008]], including the opening match and a semi-final. Its current capacity is 38,512, but is reduced to nearly 36,000 seats for UEFA competitions.<ref name="2016 host"/>

After Basel got transferred to the Europa League after losing to [[Maccabi Tel Aviv F.C.|Maccabi Tel Aviv]] on away goals, Basel had the chance to become the first team to win the competition in home ground since [[Feyenoord]] in [[2002 UEFA Cup Final|2002]], and the first to win it under the Europa League banner. This possibility ended after Basel was eliminated in the round of 16 by [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2017207--sevilla-vs-basel/|title=UEFA Europa League|work=[[UEFA]]}}</ref>

==Background==
The [[UEFA Cup]] was an annual football club competition organised by [[UEFA]] since 1971 for eligible European football clubs. Clubs qualified for the competition based on their performance in their national leagues and cup competitions. It was the second-tier competition of European club football, ranking below the [[UEFA Champions League]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/feb/14/europa-league-is-back-more-than-ever-competition-to-savour-round-of-32-arsenal|title=The Europa League is back and more than ever is a competition to savour |work=The Guardian |first=Sachin |last=Nakrani |date=14 February 2018 |access-date=11 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191129004712/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/feb/14/europa-league-is-back-more-than-ever-competition-to-savour-round-of-32-arsenal |archive-date=29 November 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2010, the UEFA Cup evolved into the Europa League.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/europa-league/5344380/Europa-League-Guide-to-Uefa-Cups-replacement-tournament.html |work=The Daily Telegraph |title=Europa League: Guide to Uefa Cup's replacement tournament |access-date=11 May 2020 |date=19 May 2009 |first=John |last=Ley |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170531010253/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/europa-league/5344380/Europa-League-Guide-to-Uefa-Cups-replacement-tournament.html |archive-date=31 May 2017 |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref>

Liverpool's first appearance in [[Liverpool F.C. in European football|European football]] was in the [[1964–65 European Cup]], when they reached the semi-finals. Since then, they had appeared in eleven European finals with five European Cup triumphs ([[1977 European Cup Final|1977]], [[1978 European Cup Final|1978]], [[1981 European Cup Final|1981]], [[1984 European Cup Final|1984]], [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final|2005]]), two losses in [[1985 European Cup Final|1985]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League Final|2007]], three UEFA Cup victories in [[1973 UEFA Cup Final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup Final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Cup Final|2001]] and defeats in the final of the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners's Cup]] in [[1966 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1966]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d8121d9757f-df0e18998db6-1000--liverpool-s-three-uefa-cup-wins/|title=Liverpool's three UEFA Cup wins|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=16 May 2016}}</ref> Sevilla had first appeared in [[Sevilla FC in European football|European competition]] in the [[1957–58 European Cup]] when they reached the quarter-finals. This represented Sevilla's best performance until they won the first of their four Europa League victories in [[2006 UEFA Cup Final|2006]]. They had subsequently won in [[2007 UEFA Cup Final|2007]], [[2014 UEFA Europa League Final|2014]], and [[2015 UEFA Europa League Final|2015]]. This was their third final in succession.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d817c7c9a5e-5cb61ff697a3-1000--sevilla-s-first-four-final-successes/|title=Sevilla's first four final successes|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=16 May 2016}}</ref> Victory would secure a record fifth success in the competition, while victory for Liverpool would see them join Sevilla as the most successful team in the competition with four victories. This was the first time the two sides had met each other in European competition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0253-0d8121de722b-c63bd51deb91-1000/ |title=Liverpool v Sevilla match background |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=11 May 2016 |access-date=19 May 2020 }}</ref>

The [[goal-line technology]] system [[Hawk-Eye]] was used for the match. This was the first UEFA competition match to employ goal-line technology, following approval by the UEFA Executive Committee in January 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35726091|title=Goalline technology to be used in Champions League final|publisher=BBC Sport|date=4 March 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/022d-0e94575d1dd6-1a53b3db9720-1000--goal-line-technology-for-europa-league-final/|title=Goal-line technology for Europa League final|publisher=[[UEFA]]|date=17 May 2016}}</ref>

