===Liverpool===
[[File:Match de football Bordeaux Liverpool le 17 septembre 2015 01.jpg|right|250px|thumb|Liverpool's first match in the group stage against Bordeaux ended in a 1–1 draw.]]
Liverpool were drawn in Group B along with [[FC Rubin Kazan|Rubin Kazan]] of Russia, [[FC Girondins de Bordeaux|Bordeaux]] of France, and [[FC Sion|Sion]] of Switzerland. Their first match was away against Bordeaux at the [[Nouveau Stade de Bordeaux]]. [[Adam Lallana]] scored for Liverpool in the 65th minute but Bordeaux equalised in the 81st minute when [[Jussiê]] to secure a 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34203664 |title=Bordeaux 1–1 Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=17 September 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Saj |last=Chowdhury }}</ref> Their next match again at their home ground, Anfield against Sion finished by the same scoreline. A fourth minute goal by Lallana was followed by an [[Ebenezer Assifuah]] goal for Sion in the 18th minute.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2015/oct/01/liverpool-v-fc-sion-europa-league-live |title=Liverpool 1-1 FC Sion: Europa League – as it happened |work=The Guardian |date=1 October 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Simon |last=Burnton }}</ref> Liverpool's next match was against Rubin Kazan at Anfield. Before the match, manager [[Brendan Rodgers]] had been sacked and replaced by [[Jürgen Klopp]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34469429 |title=Liverpool: Jurgen Klopp confirmed as manager on £15m Anfield deal |website=BBC Sport |date=8 October 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Ben |last=Smith }}</ref> Despite being the more attacking of the two teams, Liverpool 35 shots to Kazan's 5, the match finished in another 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34129111 |title=Liverpool 1–1 Rubin Kazan |website=BBC Sport |date=22 October 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Gary |last=Rose }}</ref> Liverpool faced Kazan again in the return match at the [[Kazan Arena]]. A goal by [[Jordan Ibe]] in the 52nd minute secured their first win of the season in the competition.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34719366 |title=Rubin Kazan 0–1 Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=5 November 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Andy |last=Cryer }}</ref> Liverpool faced Bordeaux at anfield in their next match. they went behind in the 33rd minute when [[Henri Saivet]] scored but a [[Penalty kick (association football)|Penalty kick]] by [[James Milner]] and a goal by [[Christian Benteke]] in first-half stoppage time secured a 2–1 victory which meant Liverpool would progress to the knockout stages of the competition.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34896678 |title=Liverpool 2–1 Bordeaux |website=BBC Sport |date=26 November 2015 |access-date=31 May 2020 |first=Neil |last=Jonhston }}</ref> Their final match in the group stage, away at Sion, finished in a 0–0 draw. The result meant Liverpool finished top of Group B with 10 points.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35055770 |title=Sion 0–0 Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=10 December 2015 |access-date=31 May 2015 |first=Luke |last=Reddy }}</ref>

Liverpool were drawn against [[FC Augsburg]] of Germany in the round of 32.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/liverpool-to-face-augsburg-in-europa-league-a6772536.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/liverpool-to-face-augsburg-in-europa-league-a6772536.html |archive-date=2022-05-01 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Liverpool to face Augsburg in Europa League |work=The Independent |date=14 December 2015 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Tom |last=Sheen }}{{cbignore}}</ref> The first leg was held at Augsburg home ground, the [[Augsburg Arena]], finished 0–0 in what Andy Hunter, writing in ''[[The Guardian]]'', described as a "tame goalless draw."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/feb/18/augsburg-liverpool-europa-league-match-report-daniel-sturridge |title=Daniel Sturridge fluffs his lines but Liverpool earn draw at Augsburg |work=The Guardian |date=18 February 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Andy |last=Hunter }}</ref> Liverpool won the second leg at Anfield 1–0 courtesy of a penalty from Milner.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/feb/25/liverpool-augsburg-europa-league-match-report |title=Liverpool edge past Augsburg thanks to James Milner penalty |work=The Guardian |date=25 February 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Andy |last=Hunter }}</ref> Liverpool were drawn against domestic rivals [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in the round of 16. Liverpool went ahead in the first leg at Anfield in the 2oth minute when [[Daniel Sturridge]] converted a penalty after [[Nathaniel Clyne]] was fouled in the United penalty area by [[Memphis Depay]]. [[Roberto Firmino]] scored in the 73rd minute to secure a 2–0 victory.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35763810 |title=Liverpool 2–0 Manchester United |website=BBC Sport |date=10 March 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Phil |last=McNulty }}</ref> The second leg at United's home ground, [[Old Trafford]], saw United go ahead in the 32nd minute when [[Anthony Martial]] scored a penalty following a foul by Clyne. Liverpool equalised in the 45th minute when [[Philippe Coutinho]] scored and with no further goals, the match ended 1–1 with Liverpool's progression to the quarter-finals with a 3–1 aggregate victory. 

Their next opponents were [[Borussia Dortmund]] of Germany, the team Liverpool manager Klopp had managed the season before. The first leg at Dortmund's home ground, the [[Westfalenstadion]], saw Liverpool take the lead in the 36th minute when [[Divock Origi]] scored. The lead was shortlived as [[Mats Hummels]] equalised for Dortmund in the 38th minute to secure a 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35980287 |title=Dortmund 1–1 Liverpool |website=BBC Sport |date=7 April 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Gary |last=Rose }}</ref> Dortmund scored two early goals in the second leg at Anfield courtesy of [[Henrikh Mkhitaryan]] and [[Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang]]. The first half finished with Dortmund two goals up which meant Liverpool needed to score three to progress due to the [[away goals rule]]. Liverpool scored early on in the second half when Origi, the lead was shortlived as [[Marco Reus]] scored for Dortmund nine minutes later. Once again, Liverpool needed to score three goals to progress. Coutinho scored in the 66th minute and Liverpool levelled the match 11 minutes later when [[Mamadou Sakho]]. Still requiring one goal to progress to the semi-finals, [[Dejan Lovren]] headed in Milner's [[Cross (association football)|cross]] in stoppage time to win the match 4–3 and the tie 5–4 on aggregate. The match was described by [[Phil McNulty]] of [[BBC Sport]], as "one of the most dramatic comebacks Anfield has seen."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36020910 |title=Liverpool 4–3 Dortmund |website=BBC Sport |date=14 April 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Phil |last=McNulty }}</ref> Liverpool faced [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] of Spain in the semi-finals. The first leg at Villarreal's home ground, [[El Madrigal]], was on course to finish goalless until [[Adrián López]] scored in the last minute to secure a 1–0 victory for Villarreal. Liverpool went ahead in the seventh minute of the second leg at Anfield when [[Bruno Soriano]] scored an [[Own goal#Association football|own goal]]. Sturridge extended their lead in the 63rd minute and a goal from Lallana in the 81st minute, following a [[Penalty card#Red card|red card]] for [[Víctor Ruiz (footballer, born 1989)|Víctor Ruiz]], secured a 3–0 win and Liverpool's progression to the final.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36126028 |title=Liverpool 3–0 Villarreal |website=BBC Sport |date=5 May 2016 |access-date=1 June 2020 |first=Alistair |last=Magowan }}</ref>

==Pre-match==

===Ambassador===
[[File:Alex_Frei.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Alexander Frei]]]]
Former [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]] and [[FC Basel|Basel]] striker [[Alexander Frei]] was named the ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite news|title=Former Swiss soccer player Alexander Frei, ambassador for the UEFA Europa League final in Basel, shows a ticket with Czech soccer team AC Sparta Praha during the draw of the round of 16 of the UEFA Europa League 2015/16 soccer at the UEFA headquarters, in Nyon, Switzerland, Friday, Feb. 26, 2016. (Jean-Christophe Bott/Keystone via AP)|url=https://news.yahoo.com/photos/former-swiss-soccer-player-alexander-frei-ambassador-uefa-photo-130218962.html?nf=1|access-date=20 May 2016|agency=AP|publisher=Yahoo!|date=26 February 2016}}</ref>

===Identity===
UEFA unveiled the brand identity of the final on 28 August 2015 in [[Monaco]] ahead of the group stage draw. The logo features the Basel landmark Marktplatz.<ref>{{cite news |title=New UEFA Europa League brand identity revealed |url=http://www.uefa.org/about-uefa/administration/marketing/news/newsid=2275141.html |website=UEFA.org |date=28 August 2015 }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 35,000, a total amount of 27,000 tickets were available to fans and the general public, with the two finalist teams receiving 9,000 tickets each and with 9,000 tickets being available for sale to fans worldwide via UEFA.com from 23 February to 21 March 2016 in four price categories: CHF 180, CHF 120, CHF 90, and CHF 50. The remaining tickets were allocated to the local organising committee, UEFA and national associations, commercial partners and broadcasters, and to serve the corporate hospitality programme.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.org/mediaservices/newsid=2337164.html|title=2016 UEFA Champions League final ticket sales launch|publisher=UEFA.org|date=1 March 2016|access-date=10 May 2016|archive-date=7 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160607170925/http://www.uefa.org/mediaservices/newsid=2337164.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

UEFA was forced to defend the choice of St. Jakob-Park, which is the second smallest venue to host the competition's final, as the final competition venue after Liverpool and Sevilla were confirmed as the finalists.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36228071|title=Europa League: Uefa defends St. Jakob-Park for Liverpool v Sevilla final|publisher=BBC Sport|date=6 May 2016}}</ref> Liverpool manager [[Jürgen Klopp]], who initially told the supporters to travel to Basel even without a ticket, later backtracked from his suggestion.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36244282|title=Liverpool: Jurgen Klopp tells ticketless fans not to go to Europa League final|publisher=BBC Sport|date=8 May 2016}}</ref>

===Officials===
In May 2016, Swedish [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Jonas Eriksson (referee)|Jonas Eriksson]] was selected to supervise the final. He was joined by fellow Swedish officials Mathias Klasenius and Daniel Wärnmark as [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], [[Stefan Johannesson]] and [[Markus Strömbergsson]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Additional assistant referee|additional assistant referees]], Mehmet Culum as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official and reserve assistant referee|reserve assistant referee]], and Norwegian official [[Svein Oddvar Moen]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].<ref name="Eriksson">{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/022d-0e944b9df425-e50742c1a0a0-1000--jonas-eriksson-to-referee-europa-league-final|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108191726/https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/022d-0e944b9df425-e50742c1a0a0-1000--jonas-eriksson-to-referee-europa-league-final/|url-status=dead|archive-date=November 8, 2020|title=Jonas Eriksson to referee Europa League Final}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
In the 35th minute [[Daniel Sturridge]] scored with a shot using the outside of his left foot from out on the left after a pass from [[Philippe Coutinho]]. Seventeen seconds into the second half [[Kevin Gameiro]] made it 1–1 when he scored three yards out after a cross from the right by [[Mariano (footballer, born 1986)|Mariano]] who got past [[Alberto Moreno]] by putting the ball between his legs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2016/05/18/liverpool-1-sevilla-3-jurgen-klopps-side-unravel-against-rampant/|title=Liverpool 1 Sevilla 3: Jurgen Klopp's side unravel against rampant Spaniards in Europa League final|date=19 May 2016|work=Daily Telegraph|access-date=5 August 2016}}</ref>

Sevilla missed two more chances to score before they went in front in the 64th minute when [[Coke (footballer)|Coke]] scored with a low right foot shot to the corner of the net from twenty yards. Coke got his second in the 70th minute with a right foot shot from six yards out on the right after the ball broke to him via a deflection off a Liverpool player.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/18/liverpool-v-sevilla-europa-league-final-live|title=Sevilla beat Liverpool 3–1 to claim Europa League title – as it happened|date=19 May 2016|work=Guardian|access-date=5 August 2016}}</ref>

With this defeat, Liverpool became the second English club and fourth overall – after [[Hamburger SV]], [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] and [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] – to have been runner-up in [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the three main European club competitions|all three major European competitions]] ([[UEFA Champions League|European Champion Clubs' Cup/UEFA Champions League]], UEFA Cup/UEFA Europa League, and the now-defunct [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]]).<ref>{{cite web|author=Roberto Di Maggio|url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/intfinalrec.html|title=International Finalists|work=Record Sport Soccer Statistics Foundation|date=18 February 2021}}</ref>

===Details===
The "home" team (for administrative purposes) was determined by an additional draw held after the semi-final draw, which was held on 15 April 2016 at UEFA headquarters in [[Nyon]], [[Switzerland]].<ref name="drawSF_1">{{cite web |title=Draws — Semi-finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/draws/2016/2000662/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=15 April 2016 }}</ref>

